{
 "_what": "The Freedom Trail measured as a WALK. 16 published stop coordinates from trails.json, routed on brouter.de profile hiking-beta (a true foot router, unlike router.project-osrm.org which is a car and was caught on 2026-09-03), then simplified to a 30 m minimum vertex spacing, then given an elevation per vertex from USGS 3DEP at epqs.nationalmap.gov, units Feet.",
 "_measured": "2026-09-03",
 "_length_m": 5650.8,
 "_length_mi": 3.511,
 "_published_length_mi": 2.5,
 "_disagreement": "The Freedom Trail Foundation publishes 2.5 miles. This measurement says 3.511. The gap is not a rounding: the crow-flies sum through the 16 stop coordinates is already 3537 m (2.198 mi), and one of those crow lines, Copp's Hill to the USS Constitution, runs 566 m straight over the Charles River, which no walker does. Replace that single leg with its routed 1487 m and leave every other leg at its impossible straight line, and the floor is still 4458 m, 2.77 mi. So 2.5 miles is below the shortest path that can visit these stops. Say both numbers on any page; do not average them and do not quietly print the published one.",
 "_columns": [
  "x_m_east",
  "y_m_north",
  "elev_ft",
  "cum_m"
 ],
 "_origin_lonlat": [
  -71.066031,
  42.354988
 ],
 "_elev_carried_not_measured": "3DEP is a BARE EARTH raster, so over a bridge or a pier it returns the water, not the deck. Two places came back below sea level and both are real structures: vertices 90-92 (3862-3933 m) are the Charlestown Bridge, and vertex 108 (4863 m) is the USS Constitution's berth, which is stop 15 itself. Their elevations are CARRIED from the vertices on either side, and are therefore not measured. Everything else is.",
